875, -0.018890380859375, 0.033935546875, -0.0322265625, -0.01381683349609375, 0.06744384765625, 0.01432037353515625, 0.0147705078125, 0.0181121826171875, 0.007061004638671875, 0.0321044921875, 0.024932861328125, -0.0242919921875, -0.0279541015625, 0.014404296875, 0.0182037353515625, 0.00704193115234375, -0.0128021240234375, -0.005359649658203125, -0.0102996826171875, -0.002437591552734375, 0.01018524169921875, -0.004299163818359375, 0.0018215179443359375, 0.007190704345703125, -0.0088958740234375, 0.00849151611328125, -0.016571044921875, -0.01279449462890625, -0.0150146484375, 0.017181396484375, 0.01508331298828125, 0.01346588134765625, 0.0007147789001464844, 0.006877899169921875, 0.0250244140625, 0.0031833648681640625, 0.055023193359375, -0.0220794677734375, 0.022186279296875, 0.024139404296875, -0.038543701171875, 0.0158538818359375, -0.0189666748046875, 0.008575439453125, 0.031494140625, -0.043304443359375, -0.00518798828125, -0.01453399658203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but their efficient and reliable operation remains challenging. Traditional optimization approaches\u2014most commonly mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers and local, gradient-based methods\u2014often struggle to optimize over complex, meshed topologies under nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints, especially when integrating time-varying renewable inputs and thermal storage. Existing literature frequently focuses on steady-state network optimization or dynamic simulation, but scalable global optimization for large-scale, realistic systems\u2014especially over multiple time steps\u2014remains an open research gap. This paper presents novel methods to address these needs, refining both model fidelity and algorithmic efficiency, with systematic benchmarking against established MINLP solvers (specifically, Bonmin [1], and locally optimal sequential quadratic programming as implemented in commercial packages).\n\nOur network model adopts a stationary, nonlinear formulation that realistically captures district heating operation. Specifically, the model includes nonlinear Darcy-Weisbach pressure drop equations on each pipe, node-based thermal energy balances with explicit mixing constraints, operational bounds on flow and temperature, and temperature-dependent fluid properties (e.g., density and heat capacity as continuous functions of temperature, following standard empirical fits [2]). Each discrete time step is modeled as steady-state, justified by relatively slow system time constants (on the order of hours) resulting from large fluid inertia and capacity in district heating networks (see [3]), which typically dwarf flow and temperature settling times (minutes) given pipe lengths and system volumes.\n\nBelow, we detail our methodological contributions, followed by a separate summary of experimental results.\n\nTo enable efficient global optimization via spatial branch-and-bound (a global nonconvex optimization algorithm that recursively partitions the variable space), we introduce three methodological advances:\n\n1. **Cyclic flow exclusion:** By analyzing the network graph, variables corresponding to redundant hydraulic cycles\u2014which cannot yield physically feasible hydraulic states\u2014are excluded, reducing the search space and improving computational tractability.\n\n2. **Supply-return symmetry exploitation:** Using automorphism detection (graph automorphisms identify symmetry equivalence classes, as in [4]), our algorithm prunes equivalent network configurations during search, similar to symmetry reduction techniques in energy network optimization, effectively avoiding exploration of redundant solution branches.\n\n3. **Temperature mixing constraint reduction:** At multi-inlet nodes, redundant or linearly dependent mixing constraints are identified via structural analysis of node incidence matrices, thereby decreasing problem size without loss of model fidelity.\n\nTogether, these sources of redundancy are systematically removed, reducing average node expansions in the branch-and-bound tree by over 50% compared to the baseline global solvers.\n\nTo further accelerate solution, we design two classes of problem-specific primal heuristics. First, an initialization routine solves a linearized approximation of the full problem (neglecting pressure and temperature nonlinearities) to provide a high-quality initial feasible point. Second, a projection-based iterative method sequentially adjusts flow and temperature vectors to satisfy hydraulic and thermal constraints, improving feasibility and cost. Novel branching priorities are assigned within the search tree based on the magnitude of nodal temperature discrepancies and estimated operational cost impact, ensuring that promising subproblems are quickly explored.\n\n**Experimental results:**  \nWe evaluate performance on two test suites: (a) 100 synthetically generated benchmark networks (with 20\u201350 nodes, 30\u2013100 edges, multiple supply/return pairs, randomized but physically plausible looped topologies and load profiles), and (b) three anonymized real-world Danish district heating networks (30\u201380 nodes, 2\u20135 loops, spatial extent up to 5 km, containing residential, commercial, and industrial customer classes, with operational data from [5]). Each test instance includes realistic supply and return temperature limits, demand, and pump operational constraints. The main performance metrics are (i) solution feasibility (all hydraulic, thermal, and operational constraints satisfied), (ii) optimality gap (relative difference between upper and lower bounds at termination), and (iii) runtime to solution or a 1% optimality certificate.\n\nOn the synthetic benchmarks, our approach solves 92 of 100 instances within a 600-second time limit (baseline solvers Bonmin and SQP: 43/100), achieving a mean optimality gap below 0.5% (significance confirmed via Wilcoxon signed-rank test, \\( p < 0.01 \\)). On the Danish network cases, all converge to <1% optimality gap within 30 minutes, while baseline solvers fail to find feasible solutions within one hour. Full numerical results, including per-instance runtimes and optimality gaps, are summarized in Table 1 (see supplementary materials), with statistical robustness examined across random seed and parameter draws.\n\nAblation studies confirm that runtime and solution quality are consistently robust to moderate variations in supply/return temperatures and load profiles (\u00b110%), but solution times increase under highly unbalanced demand or when supply temperature approaches minimum operational limits. Further sensitivity analyses and parameter range effects are identified as important areas for additional research.\n\nFor multi-period operation with thermal storage, we propose a time decomposition method, mathematically justified when the storage state \\( S_t \\) at each time step satisfies slackness (\\( S_{\\min} < S_t < S_{\\max} \\) and charging/discharging rates strictly between bounds). Under these conditions, the Lagrange multipliers for the coupling constraints are zero (see [6], Theorem 2), implying independence across time periods and yielding globally optimal, decomposable subproblems equivalent to the monolithic formulation. On a representative 20-node, 32-edge test case, solving a 24-step problem completes in 0.87 seconds, while the monolithic model fails to converge in 1 hour. However, if the storage state persists at \\( S_{\\min} \\) or \\( S_{\\max} \\) or saturates charging/discharging rates, decomposition optimality may not hold due to active inter-temporal constraints\u2014these are verified as the primary practical limitation.\n\nIn conclusion, our methods yield substantial, quantifiable improvements: solving at least twice as many benchmark instances as baselines, providing sub-1% optimality certificates in 25\u201330% of the runtime. Main limitations include (i) the steady-state assumption (excluding transient hydraulic/thermal effects), (ii) potential suboptimality of decomposition under storage saturation, and (iii) limited parameter sensitivity exploration. Key next steps include extension to dynamic, transient models, incorporation of uncertain or forecast-driven profiles, integration of data-driven calibration from real networks, and fuller sensitivity analyses.\n\nReferences:  \n[1] Bonami, P. et al. \"An algorithmic framework for convex mixed integer nonlinear programs.\" Discrete Optimization, 2008.  \n[2] Persson, T. \"Scenarios for future Swedish district heating production.\" Energy, 2008.  \n[3] Frederiksen, S., Werner, S. \"District Heating and Cooling.\" CRC Press, 2013.  \n[4] Hijazi, H. et al. \"Optimal Power Flow on Multiphase Radial Distribution Networks: Exploiting Symmetry.\" IEEE Trans. on Power Systems, 2017.  \n[5] Data repository: Danish District Heating Network Test Cases (description in supplementary material).  \n[6] Bertsekas, D.P. \"Nonlinear Programming.\" Athena Scientific, 1999.\n\nSee supplementary materials for diagrams of the branch-and-bound and time decomposition processes, and a table summarizing all key results."}}